  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above normal temperatures and dry conditions\nthrough Wednesday with increasing rain chances Thursday into \nearly next week.\n\nHigh pressure will keep the region dry late this afternoon,\nthrough tonight and most of the day Wednesday. A light southeast\nto southerly flow will bring in additional moisture and some\nwarmth throughout this prolonged period. As a matter of fact,\nthe increasing moisture and falling temperatures at night could\nresult in some patchy fog in interior mountain valleys and along\nand east of the Blue Ridge Mountains. Otherwise, mostly sunny\nskies expected through midday Wednesday, before some increasing\nclouds in the afternoon. Lows tonight will fall back into the \nupper 40s and low 50s with highs back in the low to mid 70s \nWednesday. A few locations could punch into the upper 70s in the\nlee of Alleghenies and south/east of Washington DC Wednesday \nafternoon given increased southerly flow. \n\nThe high pressure ridge will break down to allow for increasing\nclouds and increasing rain chances from west to east Wednesday\nnight. Lesser rain chances on Thursday as a trough moves by to\nthe north. The resultant surface front is set to cross sometime\nThursday morning into Thursday afternoon leading to scattered \nshowers and a few t-storms. Rainfall amounts Thursday will range\nbetween a tenth to a quarter of an inch with locally higher \ntotals west of the Alleghenies where better forcing resides. \nDespite increased cloud cover expect highs back into the low to \nmid 70s (60s mountains) Thursday afternoon. Storm total rain \nthrough Friday night will generally be between a quarter to a \nhalf an inch. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances continue into early next week with\ncooling temperatures.\n\nTemperatures drop towards the beginning of the weekend as Thursday's \ncold front passes offshore, with afternoon highs back in the 60s on \nSaturday. Skies could temporarily clear Saturday afternoon into \nSunday as the earlier upper-level trough continues east. Meanwhile, \na second trough moving southeast from the Canadian Rockies will move \nin closely behind, with a stemming cold front moving through \nbeginning late Sunday into Monday. This second upper level low could \nbring additional rain showers to the region, although total QPF \nestimates continue to be uncertain. Thunderstorm potential should be \nlower with this second wave, and any rainfall will continue to be \nbeneficial after 2+ weeks in a dry spell. \n\nTemperatures continue to cool towards more seasonable November \nnormals, with highs in the low-60s over the metros and low-50s in \nthe mountains by Monday. Overnight lows could return to the mid-to-\nlow 30s in some areas early next week as these cold fronts move \nthrough. Zonal flow overhead returns by midweek next week.",
